# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r2 has "isolated ∧ opposed ∧ analogous_swept → presumption_against" and "presumption_against → balanced"; mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the social subordination of women stands out as an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of what has become their fundamental law", "the nature of the subordination of women is radically opposed to the progressive movement which is the boast of the modern world", "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of a character analogous to the subordination of women" and "there is a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, far outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true, which the second allows
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-subjection-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "the social subordination of women stands out as an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of what has become their fundamental law", "the nature of the subordination of women is radically opposed to the progressive movement which is the boast of the modern world", "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of a character analogous to the subordination of women" and "there is a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, far outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true and "the subordination of women is at least a balanced question, like the choice between republicanism and royalty" is false. Is S3, "It raises a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, far outweighing any which custom and usage could in such circumstances create on the favourable; and should at least suffice to make this, like the choice between republicanism and royalty, a balanced question.", true then?
mill-subjection-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r2 has nothing from it; mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3 have "isolated ∧ opposed ∧ analogous_swept → presumption_against" and "presumption_against → balanced"; they differ when only "the social subordination of women stands out as an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of what has become their fundamental law", "the nature of the subordination of women is radically opposed to the progressive movement which is the boast of the modern world", "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of a character analogous to the subordination of women" and "there is a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, far outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true, which the first allows
